--TEST--
the way through `E0480`: converting an erased receiver to the class that declares the property makes the element write an ordinary one — `rule:types/conversion`, `rule:types/erased-member-access`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// `E0480` refuses an element write through an erased property and its help
// names one spelling that works. This pins that the help is true: `as
// ClassName` is a checked conversion (`rule:types/conversion`), and what it yields is a
// receiver with a declaring class, so the write back into the property's own
// slot is the same one any class-typed receiver gets. `is` is not a second
// spelling here — it proves the class for a *branch*, and a local declared
// `object` keeps its declared type inside one.
class Plain {
    public array<string> $rows;

    public function constructor() {
        array<string> $seed = ["a", "b"];
        $this->rows = $seed;
    }
}

object $erased = new Plain();
var $known = $erased as Plain;
$known->rows["0"] = "z";
echo Core\Json::encode($known->rows), "\n";

// `as` converted a handle, not a value: `rule:classes/two-copy-depths` makes an object identity, so
// the write is visible through the original binding's own class-typed read.
var $again = $erased as Plain;
echo Core\Json::encode($again->rows), "\n";
--EXPECT--
["z","b"]
["z","b"]
